.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1145 AM PST Thu Nov 12 2026
(This afternoon through Friday)

The Bay Area and north Central Coast remains placed between high
pressure extending into the Northern Rockies and a through about
450 miles to the west-northwest. Meanwhile, a cut-off low pressure
is forecast to develop in the next 12 hours and head towards
southern California and Baja California; moving inland by Friday
morning/afternoon. That said, high clouds from the approaching
weather system will be positioned over the region by this
afternoon. This, along with cooling temperatures aloft, will
result in a slight cool down compared to previous days.

Tonight and into early Friday morning, expect light to moderate
rain in the coastal ranges and favored upslope regions along the
coast ahead of the frontal boundary. Southerly winds are forecast
to increase ahead of the cold front overnight with gusts of 25 to
35 mph. Rain will increase in coverage and intensity between 8 AM
- 1 PM Friday across the North Bay, between 10 AM - 3 PM across the
Bay Area and South Bay, and 12 PM - 5 PM across the north Central
Coast. With the cold air mass advecting in from the north, surface
CAPE/most unstable CAPE values of up to 100-300 J/kg will result in
around a 15% potential for thunderstorms. Any heavier rain shower
and/or thunderstorm will be capable of producing brief heavy
downpours, strong and erratic winds, and lightning. Wind gusts have
the potential to reach or exceed 45 mph just ahead of the cold
frontal passage, but are forecast to be short lived as winds
becoming westerly and/or northwesterly in wake of the frontal
passage while also easing. These stronger winds can cause trees or
branches to fall and may result in isolated power outages.

Rain totals from this system (Friday through Saturday afternoon) are
expected to be around 0.5-1.25" for the North Bay (greatest in the
coastal ranges), 0.3-0.8" for the San Francisco Bay Area and
Monterey Bay region, up to 0.75-1.25" for the Santa Cruz Mountains
and the Santa Lucia Range, and 0.1-0.5" for the inland portions of
Monterey and San Benito Counties. Post frontal rain showers are
forecast to persist into Friday evening and early Saturday morning.

Please check the BEACHES section of the AFD for safety tips and more
information on the Beach Hazards Statement/High Surf Warning and
Coastal Flood Advisory.

